= Detailed Solutions

#solution(1, [The 1989 general election ended single-party Congress dominance at the Centre; although Congress won 197 seats, it chose to sit in opposition, ushering in multi-party coalition rule.], answer: [C])

#solution(2, [The National Front government under V.P. Singh was supported from the outside by both the right-wing BJP and the Left Front, neither of which joined the Council of Ministers.], answer: [B])

#solution(3, [The Second Backward Classes Commission was appointed in 1978 under the chairmanship of Bindeshwari Prasad Mandal (B.P. Mandal). Kaka Kalelkar chaired the First Commission in 1953.], answer: [B])

#solution(4, [In Indra Sawhney vs Union of India (1992), a 9-judge Supreme Court bench upheld the 27% OBC reservation, capped total quotas at 50%, and mandated the exclusion of the creamy layer.], answer: [C])

#solution(5, [Inder Kumar Gujral authored the Gujral Doctrine during his tenure as External Affairs Minister and Prime Minister (1997–98), advocating non-reciprocal concessions to neighbours.], answer: [C])

#solution(6, [The NDA government under Atal Bihari Vajpayee, elected in October 1999 following the Kargil War, governed until May 2004, completing the first non-Congress full five-year term.], answer: [C])

#solution(7, [Yogendra Yadav identified the Second Democratic Upsurge (1980s–1990s) as the social justice and Mandal mobilisation of Other Backward Classes and Dalits entering power.], answer: [B])

#solution(8, [The Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA) was enacted in 2005 under UPA-I, providing a statutory guarantee of 100 days of rural wage work.], answer: [B])

#solution(9, [Following the collapse of the V.P. Singh ministry in November 1990, Chandra Shekhar broke away to form the Samajwadi Janata Party, governing with outside support from Rajiv Gandhi's Congress.], answer: [C])

#solution(10, [In the 2014 Lok Sabha elections, the BJP won 282 seats on its own (while the NDA won 336 seats), crossing the 272-seat majority mark for the first single-party majority since 1984.], answer: [B])

